<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Venta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Venta is an archaeological site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, on an island in a coastal swamp, which was the pre-eminent Olmec centre from after 900 BCE until it was all but abandoned around 400 BCE. Built largely of earth and clay, it has a Great Pyramid 34 metres high, one of the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> and buried offerings of serpentine blocks. Described by Frans Blom and Oliver La Farge in 1925, it was excavated from 1940 by Matthew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> and Philip Drucker.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-venta.jpg" alt="La Venta" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfonsobouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Venta_Pir%C3%A1mide_cara_norte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Venta lies in the Olmec heartland, a stretch of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> coastal plain about 200 km long and 80 km wide in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, which also holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán</a>, Laguna de los Cerros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>. San Lorenzo had dominated the region from 1200 to 900 BCE. La Venta was a civic and ceremonial centre rather than a town of houses: the elite and commoners lived at outlying sites such as San Andrés, and the rich alluvial soil along the rivers gave several harvests a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The centre is laid out around the Great Pyramid (Complex C), which held some 100,000 cubic metres of earth fill. Rebecca Gonzalez-Lauck showed that it was once a stepped rectangular pyramid with inset corners, worn to a cone by erosion; it has never been excavated. To its north, Complex A was aligned on true north and ringed by basalt columns, and beneath it lay more than 50 caches of jade, polished iron-ore mirrors and serpentine. Massive Offering 3 alone holds an estimated 50 tons of serpentine blocks under 4,000 tons of clay.</p><p>To the south, the plaza of Complex B, nearly 400 m long, seems to have been built for large public gatherings before platforms that may have served as stages for ritual. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a>, up to 2.84 m high, were carved from basalt quarried at Cerro Cintepec in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a>, over 80 km away, and most scholars take them to portray rulers. Seven basalt altars, among them Altars 4 and 5, are now generally read as thrones. The humid, acid soil has left almost no bones in the burials.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Some of the monuments were moved to a museum park named after the site in nearby Villahermosa, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. On 11 January 2009 a group attempting a supposed ritual damaged 23 sculptures with salt water, grape juice and oil, and Mexican legislators then drafted heavier penalties for vandalism and looting of archaeological sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Venta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Gulf Coast of Mexico from about 1200 to 400 BCE at centres such as San Lorenzo and <b>La Venta</b>, created the first great religious art of the region: colossal stone heads, probably of rulers, jade figures, and images of supernatural beings combining human and jaguar features, as…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>La Venta</b>, which succeeded it, stood on a low island in the coastal swamps of western Tabasco.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Society</a></li></ul></section>
